<DocsLayout title="Steam">
    <h1>Steam</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        Steam can be installed on Octlitch through Flathub using KDE Discover
        or the Flatpak command line.
    </p>

    <h2>Install Steam with Discover</h2>

    <p>
        Open <strong>Discover</strong> from the application launcher and search
        for <strong>Steam</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Select the Flathub version and install it.
    </p>

    <h2>Install Steam from the terminal</h2>

    <p>
        Steam can also be installed directly from Flathub:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak install flathub com.valvesoftware.Steam</code></pre>

    <p>
        Launch it with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak run com.valvesoftware.Steam</code></pre>

    <h2>First launch</h2>

    <p>
        Steam may download additional files the first time it starts.
        Sign in with your Steam account when prompted.
    </p>

    <p>
        Your game library will appear normally once Steam has finished
        updating.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>Steam is not preinstalled</strong>

        <p>
            Octlitch keeps the base installation focused. Steam is available
            through Flathub for users who want gaming support.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Game storage</h2>

    <p>
        Steam creates a default library inside its Flatpak environment.
        Additional Steam library locations can also be configured from
        Steam's storage settings.
    </p>

    <p>
        If you use another drive for games, make sure the drive is mounted
        before starting Steam.
    </p>

    <h2>Flatpak permissions</h2>

    <p>
        Flatpak applications run with sandbox permissions. Steam normally
        receives the access required for standard game libraries and hardware
        integration.
    </p>

    <p>
        If you intentionally place a Steam library in a custom location and
        Steam cannot access it, review the application's Flatpak permissions.
    </p>

    <p>
        Current permissions can be inspected with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak info --show-permissions com.valvesoftware.Steam</code></pre>

    <h2>Graphics drivers</h2>

    <p>
        Gaming performance depends on having the correct graphics drivers
        installed.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            AMD and Intel graphics use the drivers included with Octlitch.
        </li>

        <li>
            NVIDIA users should install the appropriate NVIDIA driver before
            relying on the system for gaming.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        See the Hardware section of this handbook for graphics-driver
        instructions.
    </p>

    <h2>Keep the system updated</h2>

    <p>
        Update Octlitch system packages with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf upgrade --refresh</code></pre>

    <p>
        Update Flatpak applications with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak update</code></pre>

    <h2>Verify Steam installation</h2>

    <p>
        Check that Steam is installed:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak list | grep -i steam</code></pre>

    <p>
        The application ID should be:
    </p>

    <pre><code>com.valvesoftware.Steam</code></pre>

    <h2>Windows games</h2>

    <p>
        Steam can run many Windows games on Linux through Proton.
    </p>

    <p>
        Proton is managed from inside Steam and does not need to be installed
        as a separate Octlitch system package.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-next">
        <a href="/documentation/gaming/proton/">
            Next: Proton →
        </a>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
